configd (2.12.2danos7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * session: when a P2 or P4 test pause point holds, it now also writes a seam
    marker file <control dir>/state/<seam>-<component>-<seq>.hit (key=value
    lines: seam, component, kind, seq, pid, call_err, at). call_err is the
    component's reply for P4 ("<nil>" = it answered successfully) and
    "not_sent" for P2, so a test image can tell which seam configd stopped at,
    and that the component had answered, without journald (which does not
    survive under TCG).
  * Still off by default and still free when off: it needs DANOS_TEST_HOOKS=1
    in configd's environment AND a control file naming the component, and with
    the variable unset the pause points remain a nil check (no stat, no open,
    no extra system call). state/ is created by hand on a test image only; if
    it is missing nothing is written. The file is written to a temporary name
    and renamed; a failure is one warning and the commit continues. Nothing is
    written to commit-state.json, and the markers are never removed here.
  * Tests for the marker (P2, P4 with and without an error, rename and create
    failure, missing directory) and for the zero-call case.

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 22:18:43 -0700

configd (2.12.2danos6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * session: test pause points P2 and P4 in the dispatch recorder, compiled
    in but off by default. P2 stops after the dispatch record is durable and
    before the Set is sent; P4 stops after the component replied and before
    the record is marked settled. They let a test image stop configd at those
    two points and kill it, which a real system could not hit
    deterministically.
  * The pause needs DANOS_TEST_HOOKS=1 in configd's environment AND a control
    file /etc/danos/test-hooks/pause-p2 or pause-p4 naming the component; no
    package ships either. With the variable unset the pause points are a nil
    check: no stat, no open, no extra system call. With the variable set and
    no control file the cost is one stat per pause point.
  * The pause is bounded (default 10 s, at most 15 s), fires once per control
    file, and a missing or wrong FIFO or a malformed control file lets the
    commit continue at once. The hooks never write the journal and do not
    change may_have_been_sent, settled or the fence verdicts; they only delay.
  * Tests for the pause points, including the inert case.

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:06:13 -0700

configd (2.12.2danos5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * session: durable commit journal (/var/run/vyatta/configd/commit-state.json)
    with commit record, per-component dispatch records and dirty classes
    (unknown, unsettled, unfenced, crash_window, compensation_failed,
    unverified, boot_partial, blocked). A journal write failure keeps the
    dirty state in memory (UpdateKeepDirty) instead of losing it.
  * session: fence gate for ordinary commit, reconcile and startup, using
    the service identity and monotonic clock; startup recovery consumes a
    stale commit record in one atomic write; runfile persistence is atomic
    and the failure matrix is covered by fault-injection tests.
  * server, client, cfgcli: new Reconcile RPC and "reconcile" command that
    re-apply the running configuration to dirty components; authorised and
    accounted as "commit reconcile". Commits report an independent dirty
    component as a warning with a hint to run reconcile.
  * server: the commit-confirm timer script is overridable in the
    dispatcher so unit tests no longer need vyatta-config-mgmt.pl; this
    fixes three commit-confirm tests that failed on hosts without it.
  * Tests for journal, fence, recovery, startup, reconcile and the RPC.
  * Version note: danos3 and danos4 were intermediate local builds and were
    never published.

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0700

configd (2.12.2danos2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * debian/control: drop the golang-github-godbus-dbus-dev Build-Depends
    entirely. configd's own source never imports godbus/dbus directly (the
    only consumers were golang-github-danos-config-dev and
    golang-github-danos-vci-dev, both of which now vendor their own copy of
    the fork privately rather than exposing it), so this had become a
    vestigial Build-Depends. Left in place, it forced configd's build root
    to hold both the fork and Debian's real golang-dbus-dev v5 (pulled in
    transitively via golang-go-systemd-dev) at once, which cannot coexist:
    "dpkg: trying to overwrite '.../godbus/dbus/auth.go', which is also in
    package golang-dbus-dev (5.1.0-1)". Verified locally: `go build ./...`
    passes against the updated config-dev and vci-dev trees.

 -- DANOS <noreply@danos.io>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:45:00 +0800

